00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.760
So let's get started.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.150
I think it's useful
in health care

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:19.170
to help contextualize
conversations about caring

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:21.870
for patients, working
with providers

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.870
in the context of, in
this case, a nurse.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:26.350
So this is Kristi.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:29.285
She's formerly an ICU nurse,
currently a nurse practitioner.

00:01:29.285 --> 00:01:30.660
And those of you
in the front row

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.990
here may notice that Kristi
bears a striking resemblance

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:34.605
to me.

00:01:34.605 --> 00:01:35.760
It's a keen observation.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.788
Kristi is, in fact,
my little sister.

00:01:37.788 --> 00:01:39.540
Aww.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.832
Now the reason I
mention that is Kristi

00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:44.040
and I have this little game
we play of one-upsmanship

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.890
at family gatherings and
such where she tells me

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.120
all the things that she's
seen in clinical settings,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:50.970
and I try and counter with all
the things I've seen in health

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:51.990
care IT.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.090
And needless to say, you
know, Kristi, again, I

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.000
see you rolling inside
and out of the ED.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.680
Kristi has seen things.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.370
Kristi usually wins.

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.960
But I asked her, hey, I'm
going to be giving this talk.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.460
I'm going to be talking
about data driven problems.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.830
Can you give me
some examples where

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.169
you think there ought to
be more data in the EHR,

00:02:10.169 --> 00:02:12.390
your medical records system,
and you just can't quite

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.360
find it at the moment you
need to make a decision?

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.500
And she looked at me
and sort of said, wow,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.035
sighed, where do you
even want to start?

00:02:22.035 --> 00:02:23.660
And I mean, when all
of us in this room

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.910
are familiar with health
care IT and clinical decision

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.820
support, cognitive
assistance, you quickly

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.700
realize there is an abundance
of opportunity here.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.570
There are thousands of problems
that need to be solved.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:37.980
I'm going to give you
a couple examples.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.920
In this case, assume
there's a small inpatient

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.263
clinic, relatively rural.

00:02:41.263 --> 00:02:42.680
Doesn't have a lot
of specialists,

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.565
or to the extent it does,
they only visit once a week.

00:02:46.565 --> 00:02:47.780
And this is problematic.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.760
Because if a
patient is admitted,

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.340
gets their usual labs, and it
shows an elevated troponin--

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.800
troponin is an early
indicator for cardiac arrest.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:57.150
The question is, that
doesn't always happen.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.320
So should you refer this patient
from this rural care facility

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.050
to a larger care facility
that has cardiac specialists?

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.087
I don't know.

00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:06.420
Let me give you another example.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.695
Assume a patient just suffered
from a cerebrovascular

00:03:08.695 --> 00:03:09.195
accident.

00:03:09.195 --> 00:03:10.530
It was a stroke.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.050
They're laying in the
bed, just recovering.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.912
One physician says, oh, we need
to keep the blood pressure up.

00:03:15.912 --> 00:03:17.370
We need to basically
soak the brain

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.690
in blood, oxygen-rich blood.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:19.538
Help it recover.

00:03:19.538 --> 00:03:21.330
Another physician says,
no, no, no, no, no.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.300
Look at the CT scan.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.440
Look at that-- see that
bulge by the heart?

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.815
If we keep the
blood pressure up,

00:03:25.815 --> 00:03:27.395
we're going to shear the aorta.

00:03:27.395 --> 00:03:28.020
What do you do?

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.460
What should the
blood pressure be?

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:29.960
Right?

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.150
These are just two
examples of the myriad

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:35.090
of problems that exist in
clinical decision making.

00:03:35.090 --> 00:03:36.830
And the question
is, is there data,

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:40.220
or is there a process
to sift through the data

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.720
and arrive at a support and
a solution that can augment

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.150
and extend the clinician?

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:49.483
And I want to touch on a paper--

00:03:49.483 --> 00:03:50.983
this was a paper
actually presented.

00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:53.230
When I introduced health
care API last year at Next,

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.188
I mentioned this paper.

00:03:54.188 --> 00:03:56.073
Because at the time,
it had just come out.

00:03:56.073 --> 00:03:57.740
This is a paper from
our Google AI team.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:03:59.460
And it shows among other
things that, first of all,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:00.960
you can map a bunch
of data to FHIR.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.660
We'll come back to
that in a second.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.200
But secondarily, it shows
that having mapped your data

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.320
to FHIR, you can make highly
sensitive and specific

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:10.618
predictions for adverse
clinical outcomes.

00:04:10.618 --> 00:04:12.160
So again, I mentioned
that last year.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:14.200
Since last year,
we've been working

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.000
with customers and
partners to start

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.019
to bring these tools
into clinical workflow.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:21.950
One of those examples
I've highlighted here.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:22.480
This one's public.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.772
So again, not disclosing it
if it's not already public.

00:04:24.772 --> 00:04:26.410
But this is with
a partnership we

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.930
worked with Emory, institution
in the United States,

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:32.440
where they had an issue
with bloodborne infection

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:33.615
in their ICU-- so sepsis.

00:04:33.615 --> 00:04:34.990
Now there are a
lot of algorithms

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.010
out there for addressing sepsis.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:38.380
But they had a particular
set of conditions that meant

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:40.060
they needed to build a model.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:42.250
This team saw the work
and saw TensorFlow.

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.770
And said, hey, can I build
a pipeline like that?

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:46.397
Could I ingest my
labs and vitals?

00:04:46.397 --> 00:04:47.480
Could I deploy a solution?

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:48.260
And in fact, they did.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.843
And not only did
they do it, they then

00:04:49.843 --> 00:04:53.110
won a grant from the HHS,
Health and Human Services,

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.247
to expand the program.

00:04:54.247 --> 00:04:56.830
This is great, because it shows
that this pipeline is actually

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.080
feasible in real
clinical settings.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.405
So with that in mind--

00:05:01.405 --> 00:05:02.530
so I've set up the problem.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.260
I've now described why are we
even approaching this problem.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:06.550
Why are we building
what we're building?

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.008
And I think this
is where I'm going

00:05:08.008 --> 00:05:10.570
to shift into talking
about Cloud Healthcare API.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.538
And I think a lot of
people who approach

00:05:17.538 --> 00:05:19.830
data science, and especially
data science and medicine,

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.650
you go into it with this mindset
that there are these three

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:23.270
phases, right?

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.187
These are common to any
data science project--

00:05:25.187 --> 00:05:28.150
discovery, model training,
model deployment.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.900
The question is, for
people who are newer,

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.070
are these equal in time?

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.020
Most people who are, again, new
to the field, say, oh, yeah.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:33.290
Sure.

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:34.310
You know, I'll just
do some discovery.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:34.977
I'll train them.

00:05:34.977 --> 00:05:35.900
I'll deploy it.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.200
These are not equal in time.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.830
In fact, most of us in this room
who have lived this experience

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.800
know that we spend an enormous
amount of time and energy

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.525
in that first
spot, in discovery.

00:05:45.525 --> 00:05:47.150
Training turns out
to be the easy part.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.203
Training turns out to be
the part that goes fast.

00:05:49.203 --> 00:05:50.370
And then there's deployment.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.210
Yeah, there's a lot of
issues around patient safety

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:54.410
and security of the data.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.000
But at the end of
the day, it still

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:57.950
pales in comparison
to the amount of time

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:01.358
we're spending in discovery.

00:06:01.358 --> 00:06:04.320
Let me put this problem
a slightly different way.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.920
On the one hand, you've got
all these clinical systems

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.170
that institutions have been
investing in for the last 10,

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:11.500
20, 30 years.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.150
And you've got them over
here, they're largely on prem.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:16.180
Over here, you've got
this thing called Cloud.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:17.230
Now Cloud is fabulous.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:18.980
Cloud is where we got
TensorFlow at scale.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.555
Cloud's where we've got chips
designed for TensorFlow.

00:06:21.555 --> 00:06:23.180
Cloud's where we have
BigQuery running.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.833
But there's this enormous
gulf between these two worlds.

00:06:25.833 --> 00:06:28.250
Because you can't just rip
these systems out and move them

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:29.540
to Cloud.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:32.970
So the question is, how
do we fill that gap?

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.510
And that's the role
of the Healthcare API.

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.135
The whole purpose of
the Healthcare API

00:06:37.135 --> 00:06:40.990
is to help bridge these existing
systems with the capabilities

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.290
of Google Cloud.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.040
And in particular, and
I like to emphasize

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.950
this point, because it's often
lost in these discussions,

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:50.260
at the end of the day,
we see an important step

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:52.261
in deploying Healthcare API--

00:06:52.261 --> 00:06:55.200
is that we need to
necessarily speak and support

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.950
the formats and protocols
that are already

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:59.680
native to the industry, data
that these institutions already

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.693
have, they're
already generating.

00:07:01.693 --> 00:07:03.860
Because if we can speak
these formats and protocols,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:07.990
we can bring this data
into Google and into Cloud.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:09.670
Specifically, and
again, this touches

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.880
on this particular topic today
and our specific use case,

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.130
we want to apply a variety
of analytics and machine

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:16.805
learning services to this data.

00:07:16.805 --> 00:07:18.430
Which again, the role
of Healthcare API

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.772
is to facilitate
that integration.

00:07:21.772 --> 00:07:23.480
Now briefly, I just
want to touch on this

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.563
to answer the question--
we get a lot of questions

00:07:25.563 --> 00:07:28.340
about, well, where does this fit
in the Google product roadmap

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:31.040
in terms of offering
the services of Gmail,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.810
fully managed by Google SaaS?

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:35.580
Is it raw infrastructure,
raw cloud, VMs and so forth

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.130
on [? JSON ?] Compute Engine?

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:38.608
And it really sits
in the middle.

00:07:38.608 --> 00:07:40.650
It shouldn't really matter
from your perspective,

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.192
if you're throwing one resource
in, 100 million resources in,

00:07:43.192 --> 00:07:45.080
one DICOM image, 100
million DICOM images,

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.700
it should scale
transparently for you.

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:49.190
That's our goal.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:53.240
Now from an API perspective,
we call it Cloud Healthcare API

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.000
because it's very
much an API surface.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.650
You have this hierarchy
of your project.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:07:59.900
You control the project.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.760
Within your project,
you specify a location.

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.635
This is critical in
health care, because that

00:08:03.635 --> 00:08:06.110
informs the API for
where to store data

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.390
for the rest of that path.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:10.550
You create a data set,
a data set as a grouping

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:12.043
of multiple modalities of data.

00:08:12.043 --> 00:08:14.210
So you can have imaging
data over here and FHIR data

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.910
here and V2 data over here
for clinical messaging.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:18.860
And finally, you have
your stores, right?

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.838
Stores implement the
modalities of specific API.

00:08:21.838 --> 00:08:23.630
And I emphasize the
point that it's an API.

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.610
Because if you're
building an application,

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:28.120
you're just addressing an
API in a very familiar way.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.800
It's no different than any
other API you interact with.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.840
The key difference is
that, under the hood,

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:35.780
it's scaling and implementing
these specific modality data

00:08:35.780 --> 00:08:38.510
types for health care.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:39.980
And of course, I
would be remiss--

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:41.480
I don't want to spend too
much, belabor the point,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.080
we have entire
sessions on this topic.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.200
But I do want emphasize
this whole architecture is

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.750
designed to support the
storage of protected health

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:48.380
information.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:52.730
So yes, it is covered by our
Business Associates agreement.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:53.510
OK.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.415
Now, I love this slide.

00:08:55.415 --> 00:08:56.790
And every time I
show this slide,

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:57.930
people are, like, oh,
that's so boring, Kurt.

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.220
Why do you put
this in your decks?

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.720
And I like it a lot, because
what it shows in [INAUDIBLE]----

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.770
and let me explain what's
going on in this example here.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:05.728
But in these four examples,
it shows, oh, we've

00:09:05.728 --> 00:09:08.150
created a data set
using G Cloud, our CLI.

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:10.218
We've created a v2 store
for clinical messages.

00:09:10.218 --> 00:09:12.260
We've created a DICOM
store for clinical imagery.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.275
And we've created a FHIR
store for FHIR data.

00:09:15.275 --> 00:09:16.650
And we did this
in four commands.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:17.567
And it doesn't matter.

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:20.700
Again, you can send it
one image, 100,000 images.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.565
I take the fact that it's
boring as a complement.

00:09:24.565 --> 00:09:26.940
We have made the ability to
create this infrastructure so

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.862
boring that this is the
trivial part of the process.

00:09:30.862 --> 00:09:31.570
And that's great.

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:34.170
The more and more we can make
this type of work mundane,

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.192
the more time developer
teams that we work with

00:09:36.192 --> 00:09:37.650
can spend in the
application space,

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.450
helping patients,
helping providers.

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.722
And that's where the value
is going to come from.

00:09:41.722 --> 00:09:44.180
And briefly, I just want to
cover some of the configuration

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:45.050
options.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:46.317
These end up to be useful.

00:09:46.317 --> 00:09:48.650
And you'll see why as I start
to talk about the solution

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:49.375
architecture.

00:09:49.375 --> 00:09:50.750
But in this
particular case, when

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.653
we're talking about
HL7v2 store, there

00:09:52.653 --> 00:09:54.820
is one configuration option
for segment terminators.

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:55.930
I don't want to spend
too much time on that.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:57.950
But for those who have
deployed v2 in the field,

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:00.830
know that you can configure
that in a v2 store.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.660
The piece I want to
mention explicitly

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:05.390
here is that every single
store in Cloud Healthcare API

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:07.550
can be associated with
the Pub/Sub topic.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:08.810
This becomes important.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.170
I'll get to this in a minute.

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:13.190
DICOM-- same idea--
it can be associated

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.200
with a Pub/Sub topic.

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.780
Every time a new image
is sent to a DICOM store,

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.050
it generates a notification
saying, hey, I got a new image.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:21.407
Same thing with FHIR--

00:10:21.407 --> 00:10:23.740
you look at a FHIR store, we
have a number of parameters

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.540
that we support for various
FHIR's very complex specs.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.378
So I don't want to dwell
on the spec itself.

00:10:28.378 --> 00:10:29.920
But the thing is,
when you're looking

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.320
at the FHIR specification, know
that the different parameters

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:33.490
that are in the
spec can be tuned

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:34.930
via configuration on the store.

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:36.306
Again, the important
point that I

00:10:36.306 --> 00:10:38.977
want to emphasize for
our discussion today

00:10:38.977 --> 00:10:40.810
is the fact that you
can associate a Pub/Sub

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.137
topic with the store.

00:10:43.137 --> 00:10:44.220
So what do I mean by that?

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:45.845
Again, you have your
health care store.

00:10:45.845 --> 00:10:47.520
It stores health
care specific data.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.187
When data is written,
when data changes,

00:10:49.187 --> 00:10:51.690
it generates a notification
to call Pub/Sub.

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:53.320
That triggers a series
of notifications

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.560
that any number of
applications that you build

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.450
can be subscribed to.

00:10:57.450 --> 00:10:58.950
And from there,
this is an example

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.670
of what that message looks like.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:01.980
So it's just like any
other Pub/Sub message,

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.188
namely, there's a data
element that's BASE64 encoded,

00:11:04.188 --> 00:11:06.690
like all Pub/Sub
messages on Google Cloud.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.330
That contains the path of
the resource that changed.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:12.550
So any subscribed application
can grab the data that changed.

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:15.040
This will become a
central point in a second.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.570
Now, I emphasized earlier
that it's just an API.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:21.635
So to get data in and out,
you simply call the API.

00:11:21.635 --> 00:11:23.760
In this particular case,
we have a sample resource.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.760
We're going to pull it
out for some application.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:26.640
That's great.

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.050
Now this is where
things get interesting.

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:31.380
I said at the beginning,
one of the key roles

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.180
of Cloud Healthcare
API is the ability

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:37.290
to export data and project
it from its native data type

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.800
to BigQuery so you can run
SQL-based analysis against it.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.450
That, again, is
just an API method.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.030
You invoke on the store.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:47.370
Say, hey, take this FHIR
data, project it to BigQuery.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.707
Take this DICOM data, project
the metadata to BigQuery.

00:11:49.707 --> 00:11:51.540
And that, under the
hood, will trigger a job

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:55.200
that we project that to
your own BigQuery store.

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.600
For the case of FHIR,
there is a community

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.820
supported specification
for an analytical schema.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.650
We implement this specification.

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:04.480
So to the extent possible,
this an important point,

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.590
we are implementing industry
standard specifications

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.150
on Google Cloud technology.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:11.467
So again, the projection
is based on the schema.

00:12:11.467 --> 00:12:13.800
And to trigger it, you just
set an additional parameter,

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.330
highlighted in
blue on this slide,

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.050
to say I want my projection to
follow the analytical schema

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:21.912
based on the community spec.

00:12:21.912 --> 00:12:24.370
Last point I want to make about
interacting with FHIR store

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.030
is that all of
these FHIR stores,

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:29.433
rather, can export and
import directly to BigQuery.

00:12:29.433 --> 00:12:31.600
I actually should have
mentioned this point earlier.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:33.183
They can export and
import to BigQuery

00:12:33.183 --> 00:12:36.610
and be connected to your
business intelligence tools.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.830
So you've got R.
You've got Tableau.

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:41.440
You've got all these services
that you're used to running.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.838
They can interact directly
with that projected data.

00:12:43.838 --> 00:12:45.880
So you don't have to teach
your data science team

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.908
to use a different tool to
connect to the projected data.

00:12:49.908 --> 00:12:51.700
Ah, this is the point
I was trying to make.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:53.242
When you're ready
to export your data

00:12:53.242 --> 00:12:54.760
for the purpose of
training a model,

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.990
we have a convenient
API call to let you say,

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:00.430
hey, store, take all my data
and drop it in a BigQuery.

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:02.230
And you can see again,
through a single API

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.930
call, that that method will
take your data, whether it's

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.540
the DICOM data, or V2
data, or FHIR data,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:10.570
export it to Cloud storage
in this particular case,

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:11.740
so you can train the model.

00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:13.870
Or in case you want to
move it to back on prem,

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:15.670
or take it from on
prem and shove it

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:17.110
in the Cloud Healthcare API.

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:21.095
All of this is possible
as an API method.

00:13:21.095 --> 00:13:22.470
Now the last point
I want to make

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.345
before we dive into the
solution architecture

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:27.960
is to talk about the fact that
it's in Beta, finally, yay!

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:31.030
Over the last year, one point of
emphasis has been performance.

00:13:31.030 --> 00:13:33.155
One of the points of emphasis
has been conformance.

00:13:33.155 --> 00:13:35.910
So those are the two things that
our team is largely focused on.

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.368
And again, I
mentioned this before,

00:13:37.368 --> 00:13:41.370
it's in scope for the BAA and
importantly, for term service.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:42.030
OK.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.525
So I've set the stage for
why Cloud Healthcare API.

00:13:45.525 --> 00:13:47.130
I want to spend a
little bit of time

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.680
now talking through the
architecture for how

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.510
we help these teams
deploy a solution which

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:53.550
sets the pattern that
you'll see repeated

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:57.540
when David speaks about his
particular implementation.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:58.940
So before I dive
into that, there

00:13:58.940 --> 00:14:00.650
is one essential
point I like to make.

00:14:00.650 --> 00:14:02.395
Because this is a
recurring theme as we

00:14:02.395 --> 00:14:03.770
talk about these
implementations.

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:06.880
And that is namely
the focus on FHIR.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:11.870
So FHIR serves two important
roles in our approach.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:14.240
And that is namely, it's a
very extensible data model,

00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:15.230
which is incredibly convenient.

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:16.563
The second is that it's a graph.

00:14:16.563 --> 00:14:19.450
So any graph-based
querying and technology use

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:20.812
can work against FHIR data.

00:14:20.812 --> 00:14:22.270
And then third is
actually the fact

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:24.100
that it's also an
API specification.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:26.570
So you can use it as a
transactional target,

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:31.020
in addition to being the target
for labeling and training data.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:34.840
Also there's a recent-- this
actually came out last week.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:36.802
CMS announced that
they're transitioning

00:14:36.802 --> 00:14:38.760
some of their services
over the next few years,

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:39.490
not immediately.

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:42.480
But the initial wave, the
demo, was released last week.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.180
So you can actually
extract claims data

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.102
in FHIR bulk format.

00:14:47.102 --> 00:14:48.810
For those of you who
are paying attention

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.070
to the regulatory environment
during [? HIMS, ?]

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:52.650
you probably also noticed
that both CMS and ONC

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:55.740
released proposed rules
with a very heavy emphasis

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.018
on exposing APIs.

00:14:57.018 --> 00:14:57.810
And the ONC rules--

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:02.370
700 pages-- mentioned FHIR
a little over 300 times.

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:03.390
So there's a clear--

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.790
both data science
reason to be taking

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:08.790
this approach, and a clear push
from the regulatory environment

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:13.552
to say maybe this is something
worth investigating further.

00:15:13.552 --> 00:15:15.010
For the purpose of
our conversation

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:18.100
today, I'm going to talk about
a very specific implementation.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.020
And that is something
called CDS Hooks.

00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:22.570
In the clinical space, CDS
Hook is one implementation

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:23.920
of clinical decision support.

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:25.087
There are, of course, many--

00:15:25.087 --> 00:15:27.253
best practice alerts,
[? flow sheet ?] integrations,

00:15:27.253 --> 00:15:28.420
[? clin KB ?] integrations.

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:30.670
The point is there are a lot
of disparate integrations

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:31.420
that are possible.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:32.878
I'm going to focus
on this pattern.

00:15:32.878 --> 00:15:34.930
Because I want to set up
the discussion for what

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.590
you'll see when David
presents his work.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.260
The CDS hook is fundamentally
no more than a web hook.

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:42.300
So it triggers an
alert, an event

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.583
occurs inside a care facility.

00:15:43.583 --> 00:15:45.750
That triggers an alert,
just like any other web hook

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.070
you're used to.

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:48.690
Now in the case of
a CDS Hook, there

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.913
are a number of events
that trigger that.

00:15:50.913 --> 00:15:52.830
Patient-view is the most
commonly implemented,

00:15:52.830 --> 00:15:54.490
to the extent CDS
Hooks are implemented.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:55.282
But there's others.

00:15:55.282 --> 00:15:58.050
When an order is reviewed,
a medication prescribed,

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.330
the order-select in down,
which I put in gray,

00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:01.830
those are technically
in the spec.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:03.497
We haven't seen them
widely implemented.

00:16:03.497 --> 00:16:06.810
But I mention them in the
interest of thoroughness.

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:10.140
Now the CDS service
is in turn expected

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.390
to respond with a
payload that's designed

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:14.735
to trigger a native
rendering in the source EHR.

00:16:14.735 --> 00:16:16.110
So in the case of
Cerner, there's

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.420
a specific UI element
that gets rendered

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:19.920
when this web hook is answered.

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:21.587
This gives you, at
least for the purpose

00:16:21.587 --> 00:16:23.220
of our discussion,
a target when we're

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.350
talking about an
architecture for delivering

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:27.570
clinical decision support,
cognitive assistance,

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.845
into workflow.

00:16:29.845 --> 00:16:31.470
The other convenient
property about it,

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:34.070
which I like for discussion
and developer purposes,

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.640
is that there's an open sandbox.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.960
So you could leave the
session, go test it

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.710
on the sandbox that's
developed by Cerner.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.210
So Cerner is one of the larger
EHR vendors in the world.

00:16:42.210 --> 00:16:44.690
They have a process to
just test these locally--

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:48.202
again, convenient when you're
doing development activities.

00:16:48.202 --> 00:16:49.660
So let's return to
the three phases

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:50.780
I set up at the beginning.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:52.600
You have discovery,
you have training,

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.720
and you have deployment.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:57.150
In the case of discovery, we
actually spent a lot of time

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:57.840
on this already.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.988
I talked about the
ability to project data

00:16:59.988 --> 00:17:01.530
from the health care
API to BigQuery.

00:17:01.530 --> 00:17:03.180
So you're ingesting
these v2 messages.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:05.420
You're ingesting
this DICOM data.

00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:07.170
And you want to project
it for the purpose

00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:09.550
of discovering what are some
abnormalities in my data.

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:12.037
Connect your R process to
do some sifting around,

00:17:12.037 --> 00:17:13.829
oh, do I have some
biases expressed because

00:17:13.829 --> 00:17:15.089
of my collection procedures?

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:19.867
All that work is enabled through
your projection into BigQuery.

00:17:19.867 --> 00:17:22.200
For training-- this is another
area which I'll point you

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:24.450
to some open source work
that our friends at Google AI

00:17:24.450 --> 00:17:27.030
have performed-- there's a
whole step-by-step process we've

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:28.890
identified, which
is, again, we've

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.190
tested a number of scenarios.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:32.430
But the idea is how
do we take FHIR data,

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.890
how do we label it, annotate
it, and then how do we

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:35.520
train a model?

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.648
This has a step-by-step guide
on how you would do that

00:17:38.648 --> 00:17:39.690
after exploding the data.

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.190
And the basic flow,
which I'll cover

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.820
for the purpose of when
you're deploying on Cloud,

00:17:43.820 --> 00:17:45.210
is you take your FHIR data.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.227
You export it to GCS.

00:17:47.227 --> 00:17:48.810
You then label it
however you see fit,

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.490
using, again, the tools I
just showed on the last slide.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:55.340
And you create a model which
you can upload to ML Engine.

00:17:55.340 --> 00:17:57.000
Now the nice thing
about the work

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.910
that my AI team showed
on the last slide

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:01.692
was that that also
works locally.

00:18:01.692 --> 00:18:03.900
So if you're looking to
develop this kind of tooling,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.860
you can test it directly
on your machine.

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:09.360
Now in terms of deployment,
what we're largely talking about

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:11.165
is OK, how do we
implement the CDS Hook?

00:18:11.165 --> 00:18:13.540
How are we going to build a
solution that pieces together

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:16.960
various parts of Cloud in a
way that answers that web hook

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:18.222
request?

00:18:18.222 --> 00:18:20.680
So in this particular case,
I've used the Kubernetes engine

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.030
logo for this EHR relay.

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:23.988
And that's because you're
going to be ingesting

00:18:23.988 --> 00:18:25.060
clinical data somehow.

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:26.810
And I say somehow,
because for the purpose

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:30.030
of our example, what we want is
a stream of FHIR observations.

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.630
We all know there are
very few EHR systems

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:35.185
streaming FHIR
observations today.

00:18:35.185 --> 00:18:36.560
What you're
realistically getting

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:39.740
is an HL7v2 message,
streaming labs and vitals.

00:18:39.740 --> 00:18:42.398
In this case, I used
an RU for an example.

00:18:42.398 --> 00:18:43.940
So the question is
OK, great, now how

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:46.620
do I turn that into
a FHIR observation?

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:50.420
There is some work on our side
for ingesting HL7v2 message--

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.180
[? this is an ?] open source
project that speaks MLLP,

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:54.920
so Minimum Lower
Layer Protocol is

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:57.320
the protocol necessary
to ingest v2 messages.

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.060
We have an open source
container that you

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:00.530
can run on Kubernetes engine--

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:03.680
it's why I used the Kubernetes
engine logo earlier--

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:05.808
to ingest these
clinical messages.

00:19:05.808 --> 00:19:07.100
Now we also work with partners.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:09.308
So you may have interface
engines in your institution

00:19:09.308 --> 00:19:11.360
today that are capable
of directly integrating

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:12.800
with Cloud Healthcare API.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.570
One of them-- this is
a team we work with,

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.360
built a integration
for Mirth or NextGen,

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:20.710
to ingest v2 messages directly
from an existing Mirth

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:21.290
installation.

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.960
So that a system that already
invested in Mirth slash

00:19:23.960 --> 00:19:28.120
NextGen could easily
connect their data to Cloud.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.210
So somehow, some way,
using either those services

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:31.960
or one of your existing
interface engines,

00:19:31.960 --> 00:19:35.388
you're streaming v2 messages
to Cloud Healthcare API.

00:19:35.388 --> 00:19:37.680
And I mentioned earlier that
it's connected to Pub/Sub.

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:39.097
And this is the
essential feature.

00:19:39.097 --> 00:19:40.770
Because now, you
have a mechanism

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:44.253
to know when new data was
written to your v2 store.

00:19:44.253 --> 00:19:46.170
And because you have a
mechanism to know that,

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:49.080
you can ultimately trigger data
flow, again, either directly.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:51.060
Or this is an area of
investment for our team,

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:53.130
how can we make this more
of a managed service?

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:56.040
But that maps that v2
data, grabs the message,

00:19:56.040 --> 00:19:58.133
and turns it into
a FHIR observation.

00:19:58.133 --> 00:19:59.050
So this is the target.

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:00.508
Somehow you have
this little engine

00:20:00.508 --> 00:20:03.690
running that's mapping v2
messages to FHIR on a real time

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.162
basis.

00:20:05.162 --> 00:20:06.620
So we go back to
the example, where

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:08.000
let's assume for
the sake of argument

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:09.890
and for the sake of
my diagram, that we're

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:13.520
streaming observations from a
source which may be this data

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:16.078
flow process under the hood.

00:20:16.078 --> 00:20:17.870
Now we'll go back to
the notification idea.

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:18.580
So now you've got
these [? odd ?]

00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:20.590
FHIR observations being
written to the store.

00:20:20.590 --> 00:20:23.680
Again, you can lean on the fact
that Pub/Sub is sending out

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:26.520
notifications about
this new data.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.418
So your Cloud Function
can directly attach.

00:20:28.418 --> 00:20:30.210
And why it's Cloud
Function necessary here?

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.820
Well, because somehow you
need to invoke the model

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:34.440
with the right FHIR data.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:36.600
So because the
Pub/Sub notification

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:40.830
contained information about
what FHIR resource was mutated,

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:42.600
it can grab the patient
everything bundle

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.183
from Healthcare API,
which effectively

00:20:44.183 --> 00:20:46.890
is a representation,
everything about that data,

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:50.025
and send it off to ML
engine for a prediction.

00:20:50.025 --> 00:20:51.400
Once it receives
that prediction,

00:20:51.400 --> 00:20:53.200
it can then generate
a risk assessment

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:55.840
and write the risk assessment
back to the FHIR store.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:58.750
So now you've got this pipeline
of your ingesting data.

00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:00.443
The model that you
trained in Phase 2

00:21:00.443 --> 00:21:02.860
is running on ML engine, looking
at the Patient Everything

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:05.590
bundle which, again, trained
using a TensorFlow sequence

00:21:05.590 --> 00:21:06.390
example.

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:07.765
And it's generating
a prediction,

00:21:07.765 --> 00:21:10.010
which you're converting
into a risk assessment.

00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:12.610
So that whole process is
running asynchronously, right?

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:16.210
As new data is streaming in,
that whole engine is churning.

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:18.160
But now you've got the
CDS Hook component.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.560
Because the hospital says,
oh, I have a new patient,

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.360
or a clinician
looks at a patient.

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:24.620
That triggers a notification
to a Cloud Function.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:26.620
Because for those familiar
with Cloud Functions,

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:30.130
one of the triggers you can
use is an HTTPS invocation.

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:32.200
So now it's, oh,
I've got a web Hook

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:33.757
request from this hospital.

00:21:33.757 --> 00:21:34.840
I know how to handle this.

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:36.880
Let me reach out to the
Cloud Healthcare API,

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:39.612
grab the risk assessment,
and format my payload.

00:21:39.612 --> 00:21:41.320
And I skipped over
the fact that CDS Hook

00:21:41.320 --> 00:21:43.070
has an authentication
authorization step.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:43.630
But that's in there.

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:46.300
That would be in your business
logic for the Cloud Function.

00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:47.590
But what you're
generating here is now

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.965
you've got that asynchronous
loop running generating risk

00:21:49.965 --> 00:21:52.300
assessments in a synchronous
loop to inspect the data

00:21:52.300 --> 00:21:55.630
and generate a response
for the care facility

00:21:55.630 --> 00:21:59.180
in the immediate workflow.

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.550
Again, I want a
point of emphasis,

00:22:01.550 --> 00:22:03.410
we have an open
source project that

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:05.150
shows how that
pipeline is built.

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:07.190
And this project
actually also has a UI,

00:22:07.190 --> 00:22:09.050
though I want to caution people.

00:22:09.050 --> 00:22:11.060
What this demo does
is it says, I'm

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:14.848
going to go to country X.
Do I need an immunization?

00:22:14.848 --> 00:22:16.890
This is not a great problem
for machine learning.

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:17.922
I freely admit that.

00:22:17.922 --> 00:22:19.380
And the reason is
because if you're

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:21.270
going to go to country
X, you would simply

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:23.218
consult your relevant
authority and get

00:22:23.218 --> 00:22:24.510
your appropriate immunizations.

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:26.890
You don't really
want to predict that.

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:29.130
But the underlying
pipeline is the same.

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:31.110
So we wanted to pick a
topic where it was clear

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:34.380
that the pipeline is feasible
to show you how the pieces fit

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.535
together, even if the
problem space-- should you

00:22:36.535 --> 00:22:38.160
or should you not
get an immunization--

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:39.900
is relatively trivial.

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.570
That pipeline is consistent
across all the implementations

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.640
of the solution that we've seen.

00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:47.428
Also for imaging
data, I have focused

00:22:47.428 --> 00:22:49.970
on FHIR and clinical data for
the purpose of this discussion.

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:51.910
But there's a Codelab
also available on GitHub

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:54.040
for running this exact
same flow, except using

00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:55.060
imaging data.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:58.030
So again, whether you're using
streaming clinical data or data

00:22:58.030 --> 00:23:00.580
from an EHR, you have
a consistent system

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:03.130
and architecture to apply
that's able to generate

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:05.593
these sensitive and
specific predictions.

00:23:05.593 --> 00:23:07.135
And with that, I
went to hand it over

00:23:07.135 --> 00:23:09.302
to David who's going to
talk about this in practice.

00:23:11.128 --> 00:23:12.170
DAVID BURDICK: Fantastic.

00:23:12.170 --> 00:23:12.712
Thanks, Kurt.

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:16.300
Hi.

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:17.342
My name is David Burdick.

00:23:17.342 --> 00:23:19.840
And I'm the CEO of
Stratus Medicine.

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:22.180
At Stratus Medicine, we
partner with health systems

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:25.690
to help them accelerate
their innovation programs.

00:23:25.690 --> 00:23:27.670
We leverage technology
and data science

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:29.283
to really make impact
in health care.

00:23:29.283 --> 00:23:30.700
And I'm going to
talk to you today

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:32.890
about a case study where
we've actually applied

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:35.745
these health care API tools.

00:23:35.745 --> 00:23:38.120
So like I mentioned, we apply
technology and data science

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:38.993
to health care.

00:23:38.993 --> 00:23:40.910
And we've worked with
both internal innovation

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:43.400
teams and external vendors.

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.790
And we're leveraging medical
records and genomics, images,

00:23:46.790 --> 00:23:50.360
and machine learning
methods to really impact

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:53.600
clinical care, clinical
decision support, health system

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:54.980
administration.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:56.420
And what we've
seen frequently is

00:23:56.420 --> 00:23:59.030
that innovators in this
space don't have access

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:02.660
to the tools that would really
accelerate their workflows.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:06.890
It's oftentimes relative
to data security, data use,

00:24:06.890 --> 00:24:09.690
and privacy, which are
all very important things.

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:14.150
So how can we help health
care IT move to the Cloud?

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:16.610
Right now they're in this
world of virtual machines.

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:18.770
They've built a nice
security workflow.

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:19.640
It works for them.

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.070
The boundaries are well-defined.

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:24.950
But they can't incrementally
move towards infrastructure

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:26.960
as a service or
platform as a service

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:29.543
where there's a lot of value,
because ad hoc use is risky.

00:24:29.543 --> 00:24:31.460
You're not going to turn
your developers loose

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:32.698
with an API key.

00:24:32.698 --> 00:24:35.240
And in fact, when you do, you
get things like the data breach

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:39.200
at U Dub medicine this spring,
which I was involved in-- well,

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.390
my name was involved in--

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:44.930
where somebody just put a bunch
of-- about a million patients'

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:47.720
worth of data into an unsecured
cloud bucket and Google

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.790
indexed it.

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.570
So it's expensive for
IT to productize what

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.220
the public clouds have created.

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:57.500
And it's hard for them
to support new trends

00:24:57.500 --> 00:24:59.690
like serverless or Kubernetes.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:01.730
And so our viewpoint
is that you really

00:25:01.730 --> 00:25:05.060
want health care to jump
from the VM, Gen 2 computing

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:08.330
world to a software as a
service product that wraps up

00:25:08.330 --> 00:25:10.130
the public cloud
offerings into kind

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:12.860
of an opinionated workflow
that's specifically

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:15.740
tailored to health care
and health care application

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:17.190
deployment.

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:19.010
So now, what we can
help health systems do

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:22.370
is create a secure workflow
for application development

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:25.910
and deployment, enforce
security best practices,

00:25:25.910 --> 00:25:27.482
do active breach prevention.

00:25:27.482 --> 00:25:28.940
And because a lot
of health systems

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:31.760
don't want to hire data
scientists and software

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.790
engineers in-house, we can
provide professional services

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:36.720
for that.

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:39.080
So if we stand on the
shoulders of the public clouds

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:41.610
with their infrastructure
and platform as a service,

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:45.090
and we narrow that down to
a specific SaaS offering,

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.540
then we can focus
on applications that

00:25:47.540 --> 00:25:49.380
provide real clinical value.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:50.930
And we want the
teams building those

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:52.840
to be able to iterate
quickly and not

00:25:52.840 --> 00:25:54.090
do it with specialized skills.

00:25:56.760 --> 00:25:59.260
So let's talk more about
how we can do innovation

00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:01.780
with these modern
tools, and specifically,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:04.192
clinical decision support.

00:26:04.192 --> 00:26:05.650
So for the last
five years, we have

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:08.950
been a strategic partner of
the Sun Yat-Sen Cancer Center

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:10.270
in Taiwan.

00:26:10.270 --> 00:26:13.030
They're one of the premiere
cancer centers in Asia.

00:26:13.030 --> 00:26:16.750
They have a 325 bed hospital
that provides both inpatient

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:18.688
and outpatient oncology care.

00:26:18.688 --> 00:26:20.230
And what's really
interesting is they

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:23.500
have been operating profitable
bundled payment for over 18

00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:24.280
years.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:27.260
These are five year
long episodes of care.

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:28.960
And in doing that,
their outcomes

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:32.230
are as good or better
than top US institutions.

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:34.630
In doing that, they've seen
what the keys to success

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:36.940
are in the value
based care world.

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:39.260
They need to leverage
data appropriately,

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:42.280
have really good care
management, and effective use

00:26:42.280 --> 00:26:45.430
of their resources both in terms
of what treatments they use

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:48.080
and the use of their staff.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:50.080
So the case study that
we're going to talk about

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:52.990
is distant metastasis
in breast cancer.

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:55.450
So distant metastasis
is when a breast cancer

00:26:55.450 --> 00:26:57.370
spreads to other organs.

00:26:57.370 --> 00:26:59.630
And the typical
progression goes like this.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:01.650
You have the initial
adjuvant treatment.

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:03.922
So that is your
first line treatment.

00:27:03.922 --> 00:27:06.130
And then, in some patients,
unfortunately, the cancer

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:07.910
spreads to other organs.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:10.210
There's a regimented follow
up, so every three months

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:12.820
initially, then six months.

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:15.280
But unfortunately,
there is this period,

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:19.000
there's this kind of problem
here where the cancer spreads.

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:20.830
But the patient might
come into the clinic

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:25.000
before a regularly scheduled
follow up with some symptoms.

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.790
And are those symptoms
related to a recurrence?

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:31.330
Or are they just pneumonia
or some other comorbidity?

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:34.610
So it's up to the physician's
discretion to say, well,

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:36.200
should I use advanced imaging?

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:36.700
Right?

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:41.005
That's expensive and invasive
imaging, maybe a chest X-ray,

00:27:41.005 --> 00:27:42.880
and determine is this
related to their breast

00:27:42.880 --> 00:27:45.463
cancer that they had four years
ago, or is this just something

00:27:45.463 --> 00:27:46.340
else?

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.550
So how do we help physicians
make that decision?

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:51.700
And how do we standardize that
across the different medical

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:54.173
oncologists in the group?

00:27:54.173 --> 00:27:55.590
So the goal here
is to create kind

00:27:55.590 --> 00:27:59.500
of the earliest identification
of recurrence in follow up.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:01.480
And we'll create a
model that stratifies

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:04.503
the risk of recurrence for
each individual patient,

00:28:04.503 --> 00:28:05.920
with the result
being that we have

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:08.890
effective and efficient
use of advanced imaging,

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:11.260
and we can reduce by
three to six months

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:14.080
the time from the
actual cancer recurrence

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:17.120
to start of
recurrence treatment.

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:19.680
So generically, the
process looks like this.

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:21.680
We have a DICOM
store for images.

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.590
We leverage FHIR for
modeling the medical record.

00:28:24.590 --> 00:28:27.200
And we use registries
for curated parts

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:28.580
of the medical record.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.050
We take these
three data sources,

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.360
feed them into a machine
learning framework,

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:36.370
and bring actionable
insight to the clinician.

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.187
Let's focus a little bit
on FHIR and registries.

00:28:39.187 --> 00:28:41.770
So like I said, we're going to
model the entire medical record

00:28:41.770 --> 00:28:43.180
in FHIR here.

00:28:43.180 --> 00:28:44.650
And then we'll
use the registries

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:47.080
that are extracted from
that medical record

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:50.110
to be a specific clinical area.

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:51.790
So in breast cancer,
this would be what

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:53.540
are the patient demographics?

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:57.200
What is the pathologic
staging for that tumor?

00:28:57.200 --> 00:28:59.230
What was the initial
adjuvant treatment?

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.160
And then what is their
stage of recurrence?

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:02.910
Have they had a local
regional recurrence?

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:05.660
Have they had a
distant metastasis?

00:29:05.660 --> 00:29:08.780
These registries are super
valuable to the Sun Yat-Sen.

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:10.790
They use them for
retrospective analysis

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:12.950
in things like SAS and Tableau.

00:29:12.950 --> 00:29:14.430
They use them for
training models.

00:29:14.430 --> 00:29:15.680
And they use them for billing.

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:18.200
Because when you're into
value based care reimbursement

00:29:18.200 --> 00:29:20.930
program, you need to
use these deep pieces

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:24.390
in the medical record
in your billing.

00:29:24.390 --> 00:29:25.290
So what is FHIR?

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:27.040
Probably most people
are familiar with it.

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:30.540
But if you're not, it
is an HL7 specification

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:35.710
that both models medical records
data and also provides an API.

00:29:35.710 --> 00:29:38.160
It's a directed
graph of resources.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:41.880
And these resources are things
like patient, practitioner,

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:45.110
diagnostic report,
medication prescription.

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:47.610
And you know FHIR means a lot
of things to different people.

00:29:47.610 --> 00:29:50.520
It's a young spec,
less than a decade.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:53.460
And it was originally built for
health information exchange.

00:29:53.460 --> 00:29:56.190
So some people view it as
health system to health system.

00:29:56.190 --> 00:29:58.350
A lot of people with Apple's
entrance to the market

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:00.690
view it as a direct
to consumer model.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:04.950
But I think that it has
real value for data science.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:07.590
Because it can really help
us standardize our data

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:09.220
engineering workflows.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:11.550
It's a well-documented
specification

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:15.390
where canonical data is stored
in a really consistent location

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:16.750
throughout the spec.

00:30:16.750 --> 00:30:19.370
So for example, here's
a procedure resource.

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:21.600
And the procedure resource
has a subject key,

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:24.780
which is who the procedure
was performed on.

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:26.820
It also has a key
for the code, which

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:29.130
is the identification
of the procedure.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.590
And we would like
it to be a SNOMED CT

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:33.512
ontology that describes that.

00:30:33.512 --> 00:30:34.720
And there's just one of them.

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.960
And it's CodeableConcept.

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:39.540
The nice thing about that
is that we don't need a data

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.390
analyst to work hand-in-hand
with our programmers

00:30:42.390 --> 00:30:44.730
to understand the data
to build applications

00:30:44.730 --> 00:30:46.440
on top of the medical record.

00:30:46.440 --> 00:30:49.080
One organization that we've
worked with had a very large--

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:51.540
their medical record had a
very large Oracle schema.

00:30:51.540 --> 00:30:55.170
All the tables were eight
characters long, in all caps,

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:56.220
of course.

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:59.100
And the knowledge
built into that schema

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:02.520
to get the data out we needed
to create clinical applications

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:04.810
was very, very deep.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:06.870
And you don't want
that to impede

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:09.030
every one of your
developers' experience

00:31:09.030 --> 00:31:11.490
in accessing data to
build applications.

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:16.850
Another great use of FHIR
is to normalize variability

00:31:16.850 --> 00:31:18.270
from data sources.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:20.960
So whether you're combining
legacy data sources,

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:23.510
or you're combining the data
warehouse with a real time

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:26.540
store, you can use
FHIR and its model

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:29.760
to just normalize the data
input and have a consistent data

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:30.635
engineering workflow.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:37.220
Like I mentioned, creating
structured registries from FHIR

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:38.612
is really valuable.

00:31:38.612 --> 00:31:40.070
Of course, in the
health care APIs,

00:31:40.070 --> 00:31:42.200
you can dump
directly to BigQuery.

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.990
But as most people working in
the field know, a lot of value

00:31:44.990 --> 00:31:47.910
is in unstructured data
and medical records.

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:49.800
And so working with
health systems,

00:31:49.800 --> 00:31:51.560
we've found that
there's a lot of value

00:31:51.560 --> 00:31:54.470
in writing very small targeted
programs that leverage

00:31:54.470 --> 00:31:58.740
natural language processing or
parsing, multiple ways where

00:31:58.740 --> 00:32:01.280
you can just write these
ad hoc programs to create

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:03.830
highly structured and
really valuable data sets.

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:10.130
So now that I've told
you why FHIR's so great.

00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:12.560
Let me tell you why scalable
FHIR implementation is

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:16.580
difficult. The specification
is really a deeply nested data

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:17.400
structure.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.370
And when you try and
model this relationally,

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:22.460
you end up with hundreds
of tables and thousands

00:32:22.460 --> 00:32:24.120
of foreign keys.

00:32:24.120 --> 00:32:26.300
And if you try and lay
an ORM on top of this,

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:28.760
you can quickly overrun
the database's capacity

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:30.770
to do select queries,
because of a lot

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:33.650
of that deep back linking.

00:32:33.650 --> 00:32:37.000
The spec also-- these
references between this graph

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:39.680
of medical record
models is essentially

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:40.830
a generic foreign key.

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:42.830
And so you have a ton
of generic foreign keys.

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:44.930
Which, again, is
really hard to deal

00:32:44.930 --> 00:32:46.960
with at a scalable
implementation level.

00:32:49.650 --> 00:32:52.830
So we were actually talking
about building a FHIR

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.440
on top of Spanner when I came
across the Google Healthcare

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:56.080
APIs.

00:32:56.080 --> 00:32:58.080
And what's great about
this is that they already

00:32:58.080 --> 00:32:59.190
did it for us.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:03.763
It's a standards compliant
HL7 implementation.

00:33:03.763 --> 00:33:05.430
And so essentially
we can just drop this

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:08.010
in into any of our previous
FHIR implementations.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.110
We don't have to take
something off the shelf,

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:12.920
manage the index size,
manage the database size.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:15.890
It's fully managed
and high scalability.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:17.460
And this is just
an example of how

00:33:17.460 --> 00:33:20.910
I think that platform as
a service for health care

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:22.360
can really accelerate it.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:22.860
Right?

00:33:22.860 --> 00:33:24.420
We're not doing
the nuts and bolts

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:27.890
of building our infrastructure.

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:30.530
If we step back and look
at imaging in health care,

00:33:30.530 --> 00:33:32.120
I think in imaging
is interesting

00:33:32.120 --> 00:33:34.490
because it's one,
it and genomics,

00:33:34.490 --> 00:33:36.230
are one of the few
high dimensional data

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:37.260
sources in health care.

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:39.440
So that's where we can
really extract insight

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:42.200
with machine learning.

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:46.340
So of course, we can label our
images and build a classifier.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:49.190
In this case, it would
be in mammography.

00:33:49.190 --> 00:33:51.380
And we can take
TensorFlow off the shelf.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:52.820
We can train a model.

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:55.400
And we can spin up
a TensorFlow server,

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:58.200
or use ML engine
to serve our model.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:33:59.520
And off we go.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:02.698
But the problem is that data
sets in health care are small.

00:34:02.698 --> 00:34:04.740
There's not a lot of health
information exchange.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:06.500
There are no
national aggregations

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:08.610
that are widely accessible.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:11.389
And so transfer learning is
really key in health care.

00:34:11.389 --> 00:34:13.639
If you're not familiar with
what transfer learning is,

00:34:13.639 --> 00:34:15.635
especially in the
context of images,

00:34:15.635 --> 00:34:17.510
it's where you kind of
stand on the shoulders

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:20.880
of a previous convolutional
neural network.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:22.730
And then you tack your
model onto the end.

00:34:22.730 --> 00:34:24.620
And so what's encoded
over there, say,

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:28.040
like, Inception
V3 from ImageNet,

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:31.070
is what are the features
of an image generically?

00:34:31.070 --> 00:34:34.130
Can we take millions
or billions of images?

00:34:34.130 --> 00:34:37.100
And do line detection,
feature detection?

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:39.830
And then we'll just tack our
model onto the end of it.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:42.800
Again, we'll sic our huge
team of data scientists

00:34:42.800 --> 00:34:46.639
inside our health system onto
that model and then deploy it.

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.500
But as you guys
probably guessed,

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:51.320
there's not a huge team of
data scientists at every health

00:34:51.320 --> 00:34:51.940
institution.

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:53.239
So how do we do this quickly?

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:55.370
How do we iterate
and leverage machine

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.530
learning on this high
dimensional data?

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:01.400
For us, we found a lot of
value in AutoML Vision.

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:04.610
So Auto ML Vision automates
that whole process for you.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:08.240
You don't need specialized
TensorFlow knowledge

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:11.640
to be able to train a
model on your DICOM images.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:13.400
And DICOM API also
does a nice thing,

00:35:13.400 --> 00:35:16.670
which is transcode your DICOM
images into JPEGs, which

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:18.980
you need to do for training.

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:21.230
And then, once it trains
and you find the right level

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:24.200
of sensitivity and
specificity, it automatically

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:25.420
deploys your model for you.

00:35:28.845 --> 00:35:30.470
So now that we have
our tools together,

00:35:30.470 --> 00:35:33.040
we're going to use the
DICOM API for images,

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:35.170
the FHIR API for a FHIR store.

00:35:35.170 --> 00:35:37.280
We use Cloud SQL
for our registries.

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:40.248
You could also use
BigQuery and AutoML Vision.

00:35:40.248 --> 00:35:42.290
Let's talk about how we're
actually building this

00:35:42.290 --> 00:35:44.632
in the architectural level.

00:35:44.632 --> 00:35:46.840
So this is work that's in
progress at the Sun Yat-Sen

00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:48.080
now.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:51.430
And if you've ever built
an ETL on health care data,

00:35:51.430 --> 00:35:53.410
you know that it's
a real challenge.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:55.090
You want it to be very robust.

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:56.900
You don't want to
have missing data.

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:59.470
And so how you do that can
be a little bit tricky.

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:02.920
Our opinion is that you want
to write once and succeed.

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:04.480
You don't want to
have a chatty API.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:09.310
You really want the rate limiter
to be your internal system.

00:36:09.310 --> 00:36:11.420
And in this diagram, I'm
going to use blue arrows

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:14.780
to denote a Pub/Sub asynchronous
message queue just for clarity.

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:20.720
So let's say we write a FHIR
resource into Cloud Storage.

00:36:20.720 --> 00:36:22.840
It kicks off a message
into a Pub/Sub.

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:25.198
And the nice thing about
asynchronous message queues

00:36:25.198 --> 00:36:26.990
is that you'll get
durable delivery, right?

00:36:26.990 --> 00:36:28.940
It's at least once delivery.

00:36:28.940 --> 00:36:31.610
But at least once means that
it's maybe twice or maybe

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:32.683
three times.

00:36:32.683 --> 00:36:34.850
So we'll leverage Cloud
Dataflow, which will give us

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:36.650
exactly once delivery.

00:36:36.650 --> 00:36:39.770
And that's where we'll write
our deduplifying application.

00:36:39.770 --> 00:36:43.310
We'll leverage the FHIR
spec's use of identifier

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:45.930
to query the FHIR API, for
example, for this patient.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:49.340
And say, hey, do you have
this patient with this MRN?

00:36:49.340 --> 00:36:50.750
If so, create it.

00:36:50.750 --> 00:36:51.800
Or if not, create it.

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.420
If so, update it--

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:54.950
and similar for the DICOM store.

00:36:58.060 --> 00:37:01.390
From there, when we create that
resource in the FHIR store,

00:37:01.390 --> 00:37:03.400
that kicks off an
asynchronous message

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:07.120
that Cloud Function consumes
and updates the registry.

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:09.880
And I can't emphasize enough
how important having a living

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:12.610
registry is that's
constantly being updated.

00:37:12.610 --> 00:37:14.750
That is a huge value
for a health system,

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:17.110
both in retrospective
and analytics,

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:18.400
and also for machine learning.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:24.420
So now that we have all of our
medical record and our images

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:28.460
in, we can use the FHIR
store and the registries

00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:31.250
to create a training test
and validation data set,

00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.293
hand that bulk over
to AutoML Vision,

00:37:33.293 --> 00:37:34.460
and have it train the model.

00:37:37.030 --> 00:37:39.510
So now we're all ready to go.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:42.570
Let's say a new FHIR resource
comes in through the API,

00:37:42.570 --> 00:37:46.110
or in this case, a new image
lands in the DICOM store.

00:37:46.110 --> 00:37:48.300
Again, we get an
asynchronous message

00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:50.670
that is consumed by a Cloud
Function that will actually

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.140
run our model and deliver it.

00:37:53.140 --> 00:37:55.680
So we use the Cloud Function
to look into the registry

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:57.810
and select appropriate
models for this patient

00:37:57.810 --> 00:37:59.580
and for the image type.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:02.190
We then run that model
in AutoML Vision.

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:05.940
And we use an asynchronous
queue to deliver it back

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:08.090
to the health systems EHR.

00:38:08.090 --> 00:38:10.800
That surfaced clinically
in the workflow.

00:38:10.800 --> 00:38:15.300
And what we have built here is
an entirely serverless system

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.670
that is easy for us
to iterate and create

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:19.973
new clinical decision
support products on.

00:38:19.973 --> 00:38:21.390
And we just have
to essentially do

00:38:21.390 --> 00:38:22.925
that upfront investment in ETL.

00:38:26.270 --> 00:38:29.570
So what we found that the
key to successful innovation

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:32.420
is that you need to have a
SaaS cloud workflow, right?

00:38:32.420 --> 00:38:35.840
You can't do
everything yourself.

00:38:35.840 --> 00:38:38.390
And if you can, if you can
make a secure workflow that

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:42.020
allows you to access these Cloud
Tools, all of a sudden now,

00:38:42.020 --> 00:38:44.270
number two, you can move
fast with these platform

00:38:44.270 --> 00:38:46.510
as a service things that
I've been talking about.

00:38:46.510 --> 00:38:47.940
You're not
reinventing the wheel.

00:38:47.940 --> 00:38:49.380
You're not running a database.

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:52.860
You're not taking a FHIR
store off the shelf.

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:55.010
And so now that you can
quickly create health care

00:38:55.010 --> 00:38:58.970
specific applications, you're
able to leverage your expertise

00:38:58.970 --> 00:39:00.080
in house.

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:02.870
The hard work is to
empower individuals

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:05.840
in your organization to
solve clinical problems,

00:39:05.840 --> 00:39:08.420
and without specialized
skills, to really improve

00:39:08.420 --> 00:39:11.780
the patient experience in
health system operations.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:14.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

